import "domain.osc"

# Unprotected left turn across oncoming traffic (Town05, junction 2328).
#
# Natural-language scenario:
#
#   The ego makes an unprotected left turn at an intersection with background
#   traffic in both directions.  A car ahead of it completes the same turn in a
#   gap.  The ego follows through on a gap that has already closed, and an
#   oncoming vehicle -- travelling the opposite direction of the ego's original
#   lane -- hits it while it is turning across.
#
# Stage geometry (junction 2328, centre (29.7, 197.0), box x in [15.1, 44.3],
# y in [177.4, 216.7]).  CARLA's y axis points south, so smaller y is north:
#
#      ego, lead_turner, follower --->  y = 201.6 eastbound, road 37 lane -1
#      ==============================[ (29.7, 197.0) ]=========================
#      oncoming, oncoming_b        <---  y = 195.0 westbound, road 38 lane +2
#                                    |
#                                    v  the ego's left-turn arc exits north on
#                                       x = 31.6, road 2084 lane -1
#
#   * conflict point = (27.12, 194.70): where the ego's turn arc crosses the
#     oncoming lane.  The classic unprotected-left conflict -- the ego has to
#     cross a lane it does not have priority on.
#   * the ego has 53 m of straight run-up on y = 201.6 and the oncoming
#     vehicle 57 m on y = 195.0, so both are at their declared speed well
#     before the junction.
#
# Routing.  This baseline has no turn action: drive() follows next()[0], so
# which way a vehicle goes through a junction is a property of the lane it is
# on, not of anything the scenario can request (README, "Coverage vs. the
# paper").  Junction 2328 divides both carriageways into three lanes and
# connects the left turn only from the INNERMOST one, exactly as on a real
# road, which is what lets one scenario mix manoeuvres:
#
#   * ego, lead_turner, follower  -> eastbound lane -1 (inner)  -> turn left
#   * oncoming, oncoming_b        -> westbound lane +2 (middle) -> straight
#
# The turn is therefore selected by choosing the approach lane, and the
# oncoming vehicles keep their lane through the box because theirs has no left
# connector.  The same rule is what the local simulator's `grid` town
# implements; see scenarios/local/benchmark/left_turn.osc.
#
# Why Town05 and not Town10HD_Opt, where the other junction scenarios run.
# drive() re-projects the actor onto the nearest lane on every tick rather than
# following the waypoint chain it was routed onto, so inside a junction it can
# be captured by an overlapping connector.  Driving the real controller out of
# all 40 junction approaches on Town10HD_Opt finds six that execute a ~90
# degree left turn and not one of them crosses an anti-parallel oncoming lane:
# at junction 134 -- the junction this scenario used to name -- the ego is
# captured by the U-turn connector and comes back out westbound, and the
# oncoming vehicle's own lane turns off onto the north arm before it ever
# reaches the conflict point.  That is the geometry ram(target: ego) was
# hiding.  Town05 junction 2328 is a plain four-way where the controller
# executes both movements as routed, so the conflict is produced by geometry
# and declared speed alone.
#
# How the failure to yield is expressed.  The CARLA original switched the
# oncoming vehicle to ram(target: ego) on @EGO_TURNING.  ram is not an
# OSC2Runner construct -- it appears nowhere in the paper, whose action
# vocabulary (Table 2, Listings 1-3) has no pursuit primitive -- so "holds its
# line" is now literal: it keeps driving its own lane at its own speed with
# keep_lane(), and drive() has no yielding or collision-avoidance term.  A
# vehicle that does not change what it is doing is a vehicle that does not give
# way, which is the entire content of an unprotected-left conflict.
#
# Actors (5 vehicles + 1 reference marker):
#   ego         Tesla Model 3 (camera vehicle), turning left.
#   oncoming    Dodge Charger travelling the opposite direction on y = 195.0;
#               has priority and does not yield.
#   oncoming_b  Seat Leon, 12 m behind it in the same lane.
#   lead_turner Audi A2, 22 m ahead of the ego; takes the same left turn while
#               the gap is still open and clears before oncoming arrives.  It
#               is what makes the ego's turn read as a misjudged gap rather
#               than an impossible one.
#   follower    Mercedes coupe, 20 m behind the ego, queued to turn.
#
# Timeline, as measured on CARLA 0.9.16:
#   t = 0        all five vehicles spawn at rest, launch on @go_signal.
#   t ~ 0-5.7    ego cruises eastbound at 30 kph; lead_turner turns left ahead
#                of it and clears the conflict point while the gap is open.
#   t ~ 5.7      ego is inside commit_radius of the conflict point; the monitor
#                emits EGO_TURNING and the ego drops to v_turn for the arc.
#                oncoming is ~19 m short of the conflict point.
#   t ~ 8.65     ego is mid-arc, crossing the oncoming lane at the conflict
#                point, when oncoming -- which has priority and never slows --
#                arrives.  Impact is front-quarter, inside the junction
#                (peak impulse ~5040, 21 collision events).
#   t ~ 8.65-20  wreck settles; follower and oncoming_b arrive behind.
#   t = 20       termination guard.
#
# Observed over three identical runs: first contact at 8.65 s in all three,
# peak impulse 5028-5039, sole collision partner `oncoming` -- the intended
# one.  Exact counts shift with CARLA build and physics substepping.
#
# Signal note: junction 2328 is signalised, and this baseline implements no
# traffic lights (README, "Coverage vs. the paper") -- scripted actors never
# read or yield to CARLA's own signals.  That is the ordinary reading of
# "unprotected": a permissive phase gives the left-turner a green ball and no
# protection, and the turn has to be taken in a gap in the oncoming stream.
# The conflict here does not depend on a phase either way; it depends on the
# ego crossing a lane it has no priority on.
#
# Differences from the ram version of this file, which the results recorded
# before this one correspond to:
#   * the map and junction, for the reason set out above.
#   * v_oncoming is 26 kph rather than 19.  ram accelerated the striking
#     vehicle to full throttle regardless of its declared speed; without it
#     the declared speed is the actual speed, and it has to be high enough to
#     reach the conflict point while the ego is still mid-arc.  Above ~30 kph
#     it arrives early enough to catch oncoming_b in the wreck as well, which
#     costs the run its single intended partner.
#   * follow_gap is 20 m and v_follower 22 kph, up from 14 m and 26 kph.  The
#     ego slows to v_turn for the corner and drive() has no car-following
#     model, so a closer or faster follower rear-ends the ego before the
#     oncoming vehicle arrives and takes the intended partner slot.
scenario left_turn:
    carla_map: map with:
        keep(it.map_file == "Town05")

    env: environment

    # Declared first: lead_turner and follower are placed topologically
    # relative to the ego, and the initializer resolves relative placement
    # against the spawn transform of an already-spawned binding.
    ego: vehicle with:
        keep(it.name == "ego")
        keep(it.model == "vehicle.tesla.model3")

    lead_turner: vehicle with:
        keep(it.name == "lead_turner")
        keep(it.model == "vehicle.audi.a2")
        keep(it.color == "20,20,20")

    follower: vehicle with:
        keep(it.name == "follower")
        keep(it.model == "vehicle.mercedes.coupe")
        keep(it.color == "200,200,200")

    # --- Oncoming carriageway ---
    oncoming: vehicle with:
        keep(it.name == "oncoming")
        keep(it.model == "vehicle.dodge.charger_2020")
        keep(it.color == "160,20,20")

    oncoming_b: vehicle with:
        keep(it.name == "oncoming_b")
        keep(it.model == "vehicle.seat.leon")
        keep(it.color == "0,90,160")

    # Where the ego's turn arc crosses the oncoming lane.  As in red_light.osc,
    # static.prop.dirtdebris01 is the flattest ground decal available; it is
    # not strictly collision-free, so collision metrics must discount
    # non-vehicle contacts.  z cancels the initializer's 0.5 m spawn lift.
    conflict_point: stationary_object with:
        keep(it.name == "conflict_point")
        keep(it.model == "static.prop.dirtdebris01")

    # --- Speeds ---
    var v_ego: speed = 30kph
    # Cornering speed: the controller has no lateral limit, so the turn
    # slow-down is commanded explicitly.
    var v_turn: speed = 18kph
    var v_lead: speed = 30kph
    var v_follower: speed = 22kph
    # Tuned so oncoming is still ~19 m short of the conflict point when the ego
    # commits, and reaches it while the ego is mid-arc rather than after the
    # ego has cleared or before it has started turning.
    var v_oncoming: speed = 26kph

    # --- Distances ---
    var lead_gap: length = 22m
    var follow_gap: length = 20m
    var oncoming_gap: length = 12m

    # The ego commits to the turn this far from the conflict point (the arc
    # starts at the junction mouth, ~14 m short of the oncoming lane).
    var commit_radius: length = 14m

    do parallel:
        env.assign_celestial_position(azimuth: 0deg, elevation: 65deg)

        conflict_point.assign_position() with:
            position(x: 27.12, y: 194.70, z: -0.40, at: start)

        # ---- EGO: unprotected left across the oncoming carriageway ----
        serial:
            ego.assign_position() with:
                position(x: -25.66, y: 201.57, z: 0.85, h: -0.0005rad, at: start)
                speed(0kph, at: start)

            wait @go_signal

            one_of:
                ego.drive() with:
                    speed(v_ego)
                wait @EGO_TURNING

            # Committed. drive() follows next()[0], which for this lane is the
            # left-turn connection across the westbound carriageway.
            one_of:
                ego.drive() with:
                    speed(v_turn)
                wait elapsed(15s)

        # ---- LEAD_TURNER: same turn, taken while the gap is still open ----
        serial:
            lead_turner.assign_position() with:
                position(distance: lead_gap, ahead_of: ego, at: start)
                speed(0kph, at: start)

            wait @go_signal

            one_of:
                lead_turner.drive() with:
                    speed(v_lead)
                wait elapsed(19s)

        # ---- FOLLOWER: queued behind the ego to make the same turn ----
        serial:
            follower.assign_position() with:
                position(distance: follow_gap, behind: ego, at: start)
                speed(0kph, at: start)

            wait @go_signal

            one_of:
                follower.drive() with:
                    speed(v_follower)
                wait elapsed(19s)

        # ---- ONCOMING: has priority, does not yield ----
        serial:
            oncoming.assign_position() with:
                position(x: 84.20, y: 194.99, z: 0.00, h: 3.1466rad, at: start)
                speed(0kph, at: start)

            wait @go_signal

            # Runs its own lane until the ego starts crossing it.
            one_of:
                oncoming.drive() with:
                    speed(v_oncoming)
                wait @EGO_TURNING

            # Holds its line into the turning ego rather than yielding.
            # Literally: it keeps driving its own lane at its own speed, and
            # drive() has no yielding term, so a vehicle that does not change
            # what it is doing is a vehicle that does not give way.
            emit ONCOMING_HELD_LINE
            one_of:
                oncoming.drive() with:
                    speed(v_oncoming)
                    keep_lane()
                wait elapsed(13s)

        serial:
            oncoming_b.assign_position() with:
                position(distance: oncoming_gap, behind: oncoming, at: start)
                speed(0kph, at: start)

            wait @go_signal

            one_of:
                oncoming_b.drive() with:
                    speed(v_oncoming)
                wait elapsed(19s)

        # ---- Monitor: has the ego committed to crossing the oncoming lane? ----
        serial:
            wait @go_signal
            wait rise(ego.object_distance(reference: conflict_point, direction: euclidean) < commit_radius)
            emit EGO_TURNING

        # ---- Termination guard ----
        wait elapsed(20s)
